# Git

## Branches

- Branch flow: work branches → `develop` → `main`
- Create work branches from `develop`, named `<type>/<short-description>` where `<type>` is a commit type (e.g., `feat/user-login`)
- Do not commit directly to `develop` or `main`
- Do not merge into `develop` or `main` unless explicitly asked
- Merge work branches into `develop`, and `develop` into `main`, with `git merge --no-ff`, keeping Git's default merge message
- After merging a work branch into `develop`, delete it with `git branch -d`

## Commit Messages

- Follow the Conventional Commits format
- Use one of these types: `chore`, `docs`, `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `style`, `test`
- Do not include a scope
- Omit the body if the description says it all
- When a body is needed, write it as a bulleted list using `-`, with each item ending in a noun
- Leave out incidental changes unrelated to the main purpose (formatting, reordering, minor comment tweaks, typo fixes, adding words to `cspell.json`, etc.)

## Pull Requests

- Target `develop` from work branches
- Write the title in the Conventional Commits format, following the Commit Messages section
- Use `## Summary` and `## Test plan` sections in the body
- Write the Test plan as a bulleted list using `-`, not checkboxes (`- [ ]`)
- Propose the title and body, and get approval before creating the pull request
